DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The tthhrr__sseett__nnaammee() system call sets the user-visible name for the thread
     with the identifier _i_d in the current process to the NUL-terminated
     string _n_a_m_e.  The name will be silently truncated to fit into a buffer of
     MAXCOMLEN + 1 bytes.  The thread name can be seen in the output of the
     ps(1) and top(1) commands, in the kernel debuggers and kernel tracing fa‐
     cility outputs, and in userland debuggers and program core files, as
     notes.

RREETTUURRNN VVAALLUUEESS
     If successful, tthhrr__sseett__nnaammee() returns zero; otherwise, -1 is returned,
     and _e_r_r_n_o is set to indicate the error.

EERRRROORRSS
     The tthhrr__sseett__nnaammee() system call may return the following errors:

     [EFAULT]           The memory pointed to by the _n_a_m_e argument is not
                        valid.

     [ESRCH]            The thread with the identifier _i_d does not exist in
                        the current process.

